{
  "description": "Shared time-series breach cases. Each case gives thresholds, one or more series (points as {timestamp, value} in epoch milliseconds, value null for a missing sample, and step in milliseconds or null when unknown), and the expected breach intervals and per-level summary. Rules: a sample's band is the highest threshold at or below its value, or the first threshold for a value below all of them; only warning and critical author-set statuses breach. A run ends at the first sample that does not continue it. It extends to that sample, or one step past its last breaching sample if that comes first, only when the sample is observed and not separated by a gap of more than 1.5 steps. Otherwise it ends at its last breaching sample, so no time is claimed after the last observation. An interval with zero duration is a single breaching sample. Thresholds that are not strictly increasing produce no intervals. The summary merges intervals across series: intervals counts the merged spans with positive duration, samples counts zero-duration samples outside them, and duration is the merged time. The ui breaches.test.ts and the Go breach port must both produce exactly these results.",
